      "proposed_score": null,
      "rationale": "The student incorrectly claims that repeating measurements 'cancels all calibration uncertainty,' which is a fundamental misunderstanding. Calibration uncertainty is systematic and does not diminish with repeated measurements. The student also states the final uncertainty is 'zero,' which is incorrect. The text correctly identifies random variation (10.0, 10.2, 9.8 cm) but fails to separate it from systematic uncertainty (±0.1 cm calibration). The conclusion that 'the mean is exactly 10.0 cm with no remaining limitations' is flawed because systematic uncertainty remains.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "section_id": "p1-s1",
          "page_number": 1,
          "quote": "Repeating three times cancels all calibration uncertainty, so the final uncertainty is zero."
        },
